# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LLM fine-tuning repo additions
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Datasets and run outputs are regenerated on the GPU box, and are far too large
# for git. Their manifests and datasheets ARE tracked (see the negations below),
# because those are what make a run's numbers attributable.
data/*
!data/.gitkeep
!data/**/manifest.json
!data/**/datasheet.md

artifacts/*
!artifacts/.gitkeep
!artifacts/**/run_card.md
!artifacts/**/config.yaml
!artifacts/**/eval.json

checkpoints/
*.safetensors
*.gguf
*.pt
*.bin

# reference PDFs — read locally, never synced or committed
books/*
!books/.gitkeep

# tracking
wandb/
runs/
*.tfevents.*

# HF cache, if it ever lands inside the repo by accident
.cache/
hf_home/

# editor state — per-machine, and you work across a Mac and the box
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Secrets — private keys and tokens must never enter git history
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Added 2026-09-11 after an OCI API private key (oci_api_key.pem) appeared
# untracked in the working tree. It was never committed, but the stock GitHub
# Python .gitignore has no *.pem rule, so one `git add -A` would have staged it.
#
# Why this matters more than it looks: git history is effectively permanent.
# Removing a committed key needs a history rewrite AND the key must still be
# rotated, because anyone who cloned or any CI that fetched it already has a copy.
# The only cheap moment is before the first commit — which is now.
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
*.crt
id_rsa*
id_ed25519*
*.ppk

# cloud / provider credentials
.oci/
oci_api_key*
.aws/credentials
*service-account*.json
gcp-*.json
*credentials.json

# tokens
.hf_token
.huggingface/
*token*.txt
!requirements*.txt
